# Portfolio Repository Cursor Rules

## Project Overview

This is a personal portfolio website for Jai Hebel (jaihebel.com) built with HTML, Sass, and deployed via GitHub Pages. The site features a clean, minimal design with responsive layout.

## Tech Stack & Architecture

- **Frontend**: HTML5, Sass/SCSS, Vanilla JavaScript
- **Build Tools**: Node.js, node-sass
- **Icons**: FontAwesome (React components available but not used)
- **Deployment**: GitHub Pages via git subtree
- **Domain**: jaihebel.com (CNAME configured)

## File Structure

``` 
portfolio/
├── sass/                    # Source SCSS files
│   ├── main.scss           # Main stylesheet
│   ├── _palette.scss       # Color variables
│   └── _fonts.scss         # Font imports and variables
├── dist/                   # Build output (deployed to GitHub Pages)
│   ├── index.html          # Main HTML file
│   ├── css/                # Compiled CSS
│   ├── img/                # Image assets
│   ├── js/                 # JavaScript files
│   └── CNAME               # Custom domain config
├── package.json            # Dependencies and scripts
└── README.md               # Project documentation
```

## Development Guidelines

### SCSS Best Practices

- Use the established variable system in `_palette.scss` for colors
- Follow the modular structure with partials (`_palette.scss`, `_fonts.scss`)
- Maintain the existing naming conventions and organization
- Use the defined font variables: `$Lora` (serif), `$Mont` (sans-serif)
- Follow the established spacing system (`$header-size`, `$section-spacing`)

### HTML Structure

- Keep the semantic HTML structure with `<header>`, `<main>`, `<section>` elements
- Maintain accessibility with proper heading hierarchy
- Use the existing class naming conventions
- Keep the responsive design approach with CSS Grid and Flexbox

### Styling Patterns

- Follow the established color scheme: grays (#aaa, #ccc, #fff, #666, #333)
- Use the defined typography hierarchy with Lora and Montserrat fonts
- Maintain the minimal, clean aesthetic
- Follow the existing button and link styling patterns

### Build Process

- SCSS files in `sass/` compile to CSS in `dist/css/`
- The `dist/` directory contains the production build
- Use `git subtree` for deployment (see README.md for commands)
- Never edit files directly in `dist/` - always work from source

### Deployment Workflow

1. Make changes to source files (`sass/`, `dist/index.html`)
2. Compile SCSS to CSS
3. Test locally
4. Use git subtree to deploy:

   ```bash
   git switch main
   git subtree split --prefix dist --branch gh-pages
   git switch gh-pages
   git push
   ```

## Code Style & Conventions

### SCSS

- Use 2-space indentation
- Group related styles together
- Use meaningful class names
- Comment complex CSS logic
- Follow the existing variable naming (`$c1`, `$c2`, etc.)

### HTML

- Use semantic HTML5 elements
- Maintain proper indentation
- Include alt attributes for images
- Keep the existing structure and class names

### JavaScript

- Currently minimal - follow vanilla JS best practices
- Keep code modular and well-commented
- Use modern ES6+ features when appropriate

## Future Development Notes

- Planned migration to React components
- Consider adding project pages
- Implement social favicons
- Add color/theme switching functionality
- Consider adding a build process (webpack, vite, etc.)

## Common Tasks

### Adding New Sections

1. Add HTML structure to `dist/index.html`
2. Create corresponding SCSS styles in `sass/main.scss`
3. Follow the existing section patterns and spacing
4. Test responsiveness

### Updating Styles

1. Modify SCSS files in `sass/` directory
2. Compile to CSS
3. Test across different screen sizes
4. Maintain the minimal aesthetic

### Adding Images

1. Place images in `dist/img/`
2. Use appropriate formats (PNG for graphics, JPG for photos)
3. Optimize for web (compress appropriately)
4. Update HTML references

### Color Changes

1. Modify variables in `sass/_palette.scss`
2. Update any hardcoded color values in `sass/main.scss`
3. Maintain consistency across the site

## Performance Considerations

- Optimize images for web delivery
- Minimize CSS bundle size
- Use efficient selectors
- Consider lazy loading for images
- Maintain fast loading times

## Accessibility

- Maintain proper heading hierarchy
- Ensure sufficient color contrast
- Provide alt text for images
- Test with screen readers
- Follow WCAG guidelines

## Browser Support

- Support modern browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Test responsive design across devices
- Ensure graceful degradation for older browsers

